  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nA large scale high pressure ridge continues to amplify over the \nmiddle of the country, while surface high pressure has migrated \ntoward the mid-Atlantic region. Southerly flow will thus increase a \nbit today, continuing the warming trend, while the ridge keeps dry \nconditions over the region. Expect highs reaching the upper 50s.\n\nOn Wednesday, a low will emerge from the northern Rockies into the \nnorthern Plains, increasing the pressure gradient especially in \nwestern portions of IL for a breezy southerly wind of 10-15 mph and \ngusts 15-25 mph and a few degrees of warming, bringing highs into \nthe lower 60s after overnight lows in the mid 30s. The surface low \nof this system now looks to cross northward into Canada around the \nsoutheast corner of Manitoba early Thursday, which should keep \ncentral IL dry. The warming trend will continue through Friday as \nthe cold front with this system stalls well to the northwest. Highs \nshould reach the mid 60s Thursday and upper 60s Friday.\n\nA stronger surface low/upper level wave will emerge from the central \nRockies and progress into the upper Midwest over the weekend, \ndriving a cold front through central IL Saturday night. \nPrecipitation chances look to begin west of I-55 Saturday afternoon, \nand linger east of I-57 into Sunday morning, with 30-60 percent \nchances for precipitation across the area. Light precipitation \namounts are expected for most areas with under a quarter inch \nexpected, however a few thunderstorms are possible with locally \nheavier precipitation amounts. Ensemble forecast system depicts 30 \npercent joint probability of 30+ kts deep-layer shear with 100+\nJ/kg MUCAPE, so will need to keep monitoring for the need to \ninclude thunderstorms in the forecast grids as well as severe \nthunderstorm potential should these parameters significantly \nexceed these thresholds (less than 15% chance).\n\nMuch colder air and dry conditions will arrive following Saturday \nnight's cold front, with highs likely in the 50s and blustery \nSunday, followed by 40s Monday and Tuesday and lows dropping below \nfreezing again.",
